{"name":"Good Samaritan","hex":"#3f6782","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Good Samaritan","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The subject of a particular New Testament parable, who helps a wounded man ignored by others."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person who helps someone in distress out of their own good nature."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Good_Samaritan"}],"entries":[{"title":"Parable of the Good Samaritan","blocks":[{"text":"The parable of the Good Samaritan is told by Jesus in the Gospel of Luke. It is about a traveler who is stripped of clothing, beaten, and left half dead alongside the road. A Jewish priest and then a Levite come by, both avoiding the man. A Samaritan happens upon him and—though Samaritans and Jews were generally antagonistic toward each other—helps him. Jesus tells the parable in response to a provocative question from a lawyer in the context of the Great Commandment: \"And who is my neighbour?\" The conclusion is that the neighbour figure in the parable is the one who shows mercy to their fellow man or woman."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Jacob_Jordaens_-_The_Good_Samaritan_%28Podhorce%29.jpg/330px-Jacob_Jordaens_-_The_Good_Samaritan_%28Podhorce%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parable_of_the_Good_Samaritan","wikidata":"Q261103"},{"title":"Good","blocks":[{"text":"In most contexts, the concept of good denotes the conduct that should be preferred when posed with a choice between possible actions. Good is generally considered to be the opposite of evil. The meaning and etymology of the term and its associated translations among ancient and contemporary languages show substantial variation in its inflection and meaning, depending on circumstances of place and history, or of philosophical or religious context."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Good","wikidata":"Q15290"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Jacob_Jordaens_-_The_Good_Samaritan_%28Podhorce%29.jpg/500px-Jacob_Jordaens_-_The_Good_Samaritan_%28Podhorce%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Jacob_Jordaens_-_The_Good_Samaritan_%28Podhorce%29.jpg/1280px-Jacob_Jordaens_-_The_Good_Samaritan_%28Podhorce%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Jacob_Jordaens_-_The_Good_Samaritan_%28Podhorce%29.jpg/500px-Jacob_Jordaens_-_The_Good_Samaritan_%28Podhorce%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Good Samaritan by Jacob Jordaens, c. 1616","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJacob_Jordaens_-_The_Good_Samaritan_(Podhorce).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/OldRoadFromJerusalemToJericho.jpg/500px-OldRoadFromJerusalemToJericho.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/OldRoadFromJerusalemToJericho.jpg/500px-OldRoadFromJerusalemToJericho.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The road from Jerusalem to Jericho","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOldRoadFromJerusalemToJericho.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/First_century_Iudaea_province.gif/500px-First_century_Iudaea_province.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/First_century_Iudaea_province.gif/500px-First_century_Iudaea_province.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A map of the Roman Province of Judea in the time of Jesus. Jericho is just north